;; waydroid_bluealsa -- let dbus-broker relay BlueALSA's PCM pipe to clients.
;;
;; WHY THIS EXISTS
;;
;; BlueALSA hands a client its PCM data channel as a PIPE, passed as a Unix FD
;; in the reply to org.bluealsa.PCM1.Open(). Relaying that reply requires
;; dbus-broker to access the pipe, and the base policy has no rule for
;; system_dbusd_t writing a fifo_file owned by unconfined_service_t -- which is
;; what bluealsad runs as under systemd:
;;
;;   avc: denied { write } for pid=888 comm="dbus-broker" path="pipe:[9193844]"
;;        scontext=system_u:system_r:system_dbusd_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023
;;        tcontext=system_u:system_r:unconfined_service_t:s0
;;        tclass=fifo_file permissive=0
;;
;; The rule is dontaudit'ed upstream, so it is ENFORCED while logging nothing.
;; That is the whole reason this cost a long investigation: it presented as
;;
;;   bluealsa-pcm.c: Couldn't open PCM: Remote peer disconnected
;;   aplay: set_params:1462: Unable to install hw params
;;
;; with no AVC anywhere, because dbus-broker drops the SENDER's connection and
;; answers the waiting client with that message -- a string that exists only in
;; /usr/bin/dbus-broker, not in libdbus and not in bluez-alsa. bluealsad then
;; logs "Lost BlueALSA D-Bus name" and exits(1). Setting SELinux permissive made
;; playback work immediately; `semodule -DB` made the denial visible.
;;
;; Same family as waydroid_backlight: a dontaudit'ed denial against a domain,
;; invisible to ausearch, where being uid 0 does not help because SELinux denies
;; the domain and not the user.
;;
;; Written in CIL rather than .te on purpose: semodule compiles CIL directly, so
;; this needs no selinux-policy-devel, which on an rpm-ostree host would cost a
;; layered install and a reboot.
;;
;; HOW NARROW THIS IS, HONESTLY
;;
;; Less narrow than waydroid_backlight, and the difference is worth stating.
;; That module could invent a private type because it guarded a FILE, which can
;; be relabelled. A pipe takes the type of the process that created it, so there
;; is nothing to relabel: narrowing this properly means giving bluealsad its own
;; domain with a transition from init_t, rather than leaving it in
;; unconfined_service_t. That is the correct long-term fix and is not done here.
;;
;; What this grants meanwhile: dbus-broker may write pipes belonging to
;; unconfined services. It is a widening, bounded by the fact that
;; unconfined_service_t is already unconfined, and by dbus-broker being the only
;; subject. It is the minimum that makes A2DP work.

;; No (require ...) block: that is .te syntax and CIL rejects it with
;; "Unknown keyword require / Failed to build AST". CIL resolves names against
;; the already-loaded policy, which is why waydroid_backlight.cil also names
;; sysfs_t and udev_t bare.

(allow system_dbusd_t unconfined_service_t (fifo_file (write)))
